course: dictionary
lesson: 2440
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Daisy» is a noun for a small flower with white petals around a yellow middle. It is also used for other flowers shaped like it, of many colours.
explain: Context tells you which meaning is meant. «There they find Taffy making daisy-chains» is about flowers joined together, not about a person. Check by putting your meaning into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
explain: Notice the plural too: one daisy, many daisies. «Longer, unmown grass lets clover, daisies and other flowers bloom and feed bees and butterflies.» Here daisies means more than one flower.
fact: «Daisy» is a noun. One is a daisy; more than one are daisies.
fact: A daisy is a small flower with white petals around a yellow middle.
fact: Other flowers shaped like a daisy, of many colours, are also called daisies.
fact: The name «day's eye» may come from the flower opening in the day, as the daisy grows low to the ground.
fact: With a capital letter, Daisy can be a person's name, as in «Daisy scrolls Instagram and sees Dad's new family».
exercise: «There they find Taffy making daisy-chains.» What are daisy-chains made of? | Grass blades knotted into a ball | *Daisies threaded together on their stalks | Metal rings joined in a line | Long strings of wool
exercise: The daisy is named «day's eye» because it opens in the day. Why is it called that? | *It opens in the daytime | It is the biggest flower in the garden | It glows at night | Its petals are blue like the sky
exercise: A gardener plants 4 rows of daisies with 6 daisies in each row. How many daisies are planted? | 18 | 10 | 46 | *24
test: Which picture matches a daisy? | A blue flower shaped like a bell | A round green fruit | *A small flower with white petals around a yellow middle | A tall red flower with thorns
test: In «Daisy scrolls Instagram and sees Dad's new family», what kind of word is Daisy? | A bee | Something to do with the sun | *A name for a person | A type of flower
exam: «She woke up fresh as a daisy.» What does this phrase mean? | *Bright, lively and well rested | Covered in mud | Lost in the grass | Tired and grumpy
